The case-split rules do rewrite as intended, but this K/Haskell version then crashes while checking `#Ceil(filterRef(TAIL,…))`: both recursive helpers were declared partial functions, so the induction hypothesis carries extra definedness obligations the backend cannot discharge.

For the relevant domains they are total—every `PyList` is `Nil` or `Cons`, and each Boolean membership case has an equation—so I’m marking only `evalComp` and `filterRef` total. This records their actual domain property and removes the spurious definedness branch.
