+
+  syntax Pgm ::= "solutionProgram" [function]
+  rule solutionProgram =>
+    Module(
+      ImportFrom("typing", "List")
+      FuncDef("filter_by_substring",
+        Params("strings", "substring"),
+        CellVars("substring"),
+        FreeVars(),
+        Return(
+          ListComp(Name("string"),
+            CompFor(
+              Name("string"),
+              Name("strings"),
+              Compare(Name("substring"), CmpOp("in", Name("string"))))))))
+
+  syntax PyList ::= filterRef(PyList, String) [function, total]
+  rule filterRef(Nil, _SUBSTRING) => Nil
+  rule filterRef(Cons(HEAD, TAIL), SUBSTRING)
+    => Cons(HEAD, filterRef(TAIL, SUBSTRING))
+    requires containsString(HEAD, SUBSTRING)
+  rule filterRef(Cons(HEAD, TAIL), SUBSTRING)
+    => filterRef(TAIL, SUBSTRING)
+    requires notBool containsString(HEAD, SUBSTRING)
+endmodule

codex
The artifact review found one proof-hygiene issue before handoff: `evalComp` was marked total even though its public signature admitted unsupported arbitrary expression shapes. The proofs only used the supported shape, but the declaration was broader than justified.

I’m narrowing that helper to `evalComp(PyList, SubstringFilter)`. The AST evaluator will extract the iterable and substring from any matching membership-filter comprehension, then pass only runtime values to this genuinely total recursive helper. This keeps the semantics generic for the translated construct and removes an overbroad axiom. I’ll rerun the full gate afterward.
